 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Zero Density Regions**: Standard free-flight sampling places samples proportional to the product of transmittance and density (extinction-weighted). In empty or low-density regions, this leads to high variance for gradients because samples are rarely placed there, yet gradients may have non-trivial magnitude (e.g., to reconstruct an object where there was previously just air) [S1]. DRT mitigates this by sampling proportional to just transmittance [S1].
-- **Local Minima**: Optimization of scattering parameters can get stuck in local minima. This can be alleviated by bootstrapping the optimization from nonphysical emissive volumes (similar to voxel-based radiance fields) [S1].
